 ### [GSPull](2773-gspull.md)
-GSPull is a technique that leverages neural Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) or Unsigned Distance Functions (UDFs) to recover detailed mesh structures from 3D Gaussian Splatting representations [G]. It addresses the limitation of standard 3DGS, which produces volumetric splats rather than explicit surfaces, by integrating distance-field supervision to refine geometry [G]. Unlike methods that directly pull Gaussians to the zero-level set, GSPull uses dense queries around the zero-level set as strong geometric cues to refine the surface conservatively and gradually, preventing error accumulation from ambiguous pulling directions [G].
+GSPull leverages neural distance functions (SDFs/UDFs) to recover detailed mesh structures from 3D Gaussian Splatting, addressing the volumetric nature of standard splats by integrating distance-field supervision. It uses dense queries around the zero-level set to refine geometry conservatively, preventing error accumulation from ambiguous pulling directions. Choose this when explicit surface geometry is required from a Gaussian representation. Link: [GSPull](2773-gspull.md).
 
 ### [Composite Error Map and Densification/Pruning Scoring](3396-composite-error-map-and-densification-pruning-scoring.md)
-A density control mechanism that uses a frequency-aware mask and photometric loss to compute densification and pruning scores for Gaussian primitives, reducing redundancy and improving fidelity compared to vanilla 3DGS [G]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by integrating a frequency-aware mask into the error computation to filter noise, and by weighting pruning scores with image-level photometric loss rather than raw pixel counts [G], whereas [Efficient Pruning Score](3598-efficient-pruning-score.md) focuses on second-order sensitivity and [Fisher Sensitivity Score](9664-fisher-sensitivity-score.md) uses Hessian approximations [G].
