 ### [Dynamic Buffer Management](3753-dynamic-buffer-management.md)
-Dynamic Buffer Management handles the resizing of GPU buffers that store 3D Gaussian splat data during training or streaming, ensuring the allocated capacity matches the active Gaussian count [G]. It solves the problem of buffer overflow when densification adds new Gaussians or buffer under-utilization when pruning removes them [G]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [GSPull](2773-gspull.md) (which handles data retrieval) and [Composite Error Map and Densification/Pruning Scoring](3396-composite-error-map-and-densification-pruning-scoring.md) (which determines *which* Gaussians to add/remove) by focusing on the *memory infrastructure* required to hold the changing set of active entities [G].
+The runtime strategy for resizing GPU memory buffers storing 3D Gaussian primitives during training/inference, solving unpredictable population sizes from densification/pruning. It grows/shrinks buffers on-the-fly, distinguishing it from static allocation and compression techniques by focusing on buffer expansion and data migration mechanics. Choose this for dynamic scenes with varying Gaussian counts. Link: [Dynamic Buffer Management](3753-dynamic-buffer-management.md).
 
